From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EvilMetaMonkey <hood.mj85@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:12:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 16/31] Handle ignore-policy drift per file instead of
 invalidating close

When the ignore policy (any .gitignore Git reads, info/exclude,
core.excludesFile, .dmcignore) or the config changes between begin and
close, close now succeeds. The close-time policy decides what is
protected, both fingerprints are recorded (closePolicyFingerprint), and
a non-fatal policy_changed warning is added.

Files whose protection changed are recorded per file (schema 5 adds the
nullable checkpoint_files.scope_change and
checkpoints.close_policy_fingerprint; existing rows are not rewritten):
- left_scope: protected at begin, excluded at close (including a deleted
  file that the close-time policy ignores). The before state is kept; no
  after state is captured.
- entered_scope: excluded at begin (it or a parent was recorded as an
  exclusion), protected at close. It has no before state.

Restore and redo list them under a new additive plan field, skipped, and
never write them. Redo of an entered_scope file only confirms it still
matches its after state; a changed file is a workspace_drift conflict.
checkpoint show and the restore/redo preview print the new states. File
entries gain an additive scope field; change stays null for them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
